#lang saffron
; saffron-lang-plan D8 question 3: a higher-kinded typeclass method dispatched
; on an `any` receiver, keyed on the HEAD constructor.
;
; `Functor [Option]`'s receiver is the constructor, but a box's tag is an
; applied type, so no ground row can serve it. The row that can is the
; all-`any` instantiation, `(Option any)` -- which is the only one a Saffron
; file ever builds, now that a generic constructor FUNCTION's bare type-variable
; parameter widens to `any` the way a raw ctor's and a literal's already did
; (`(some 41)` here is an `(Option any)`, not an `(Option int)`).
;
; What the row calls is a WITNESS defn the elaborator synthesises,
;   (defn __dynwit_Functor_fmap_Option [__r : (Option any) __a1] : any
;     (.fmap __r (cast __a1 (fn [any] any))))
; so the static path mints the by-value spec for that instantiation (the
; carrier base would read the 16-byte element as an int64), the D5 seams do
; the argument typing, and the `: any` return re-tags the result. The
; interpreter needs none of this and already agreed; this pins parity.
;
; 7.35 rather than 7.0: the element crosses two boxes and a closure, and a
; conversion anywhere on that path would print a wrong number here.

(defn bump [o]
  (.fmap o (fn [x] (+ x 1))))

(defn scale [o]
  (.fmap o (fn [x] (* x 2.0))))

(defn main []
  (println (cast (unwrap-or (cast (bump (some 41)) (Option any)) (:: 0 any)) int))
  (println (cast (unwrap-or (cast (scale (some 7.35)) (Option any)) (:: 0.0 any)) float))
  (println (type-of (bump (none))))
  0)
